[{"record_id":"june_update_2026-06-10__rec_000021","candidate_id":"june_update_2026-06-10__rec_000021","source_record_id":"rec_000021","source_corpus":"include","title":"A fine-tuned genomic language model adds complementary nucleotide-context information to missense variant interpretation.","abstract":"Missense variant interpretation remains a central challenge in clinical genomics. Missense pathogenicity predictors achieve strong performance, but many emphasize protein-level consequences or overlapping annotation priors. Whether genomic language models add non-redundant nucleotide-context signal to missense interpretation remains unclear. Here, we systematically adapted genomic language models to ClinVar missense pathogenicity prediction across backbone architectures, representation strategies, classifier heads, and adaptation regimes. In our analysis, variant-position embeddings consistently outperformed pooled sequence representations, multi-species pretraining provided the strongest backbone-level advantage, and low-rank adaptation generalized better than full fine-tuning. The resulting fine-tuned model, GLM-Missense, substantially outperformed zero-shot scoring from the same pretrained model. To test whether GLM-Missense contributes information beyond existing methods, we built MetaMissense, an XGBoost ensemble combining GLM-Missense with AlphaMissense, ESM1b, REVEL, CADD, SIFT, and PolyPhen-2. GLMMissense showed the lowest concordance with other predictors, retained the strongest partial association with pathogenicity after controlling for the other predictors, and ranked as the most informative non-ensemble input to MetaMissense. MetaMissense achieved the best performance in both cross-validation and held-out testing. [truncated]","doi":"10.64898/2026.05.06.723362","year":"","venue":"","sources":[],"full_text_context":"[abstract: Abstract]\nMissense variant interpretation remains a central challenge in clinical genomics. Missense pathogenicity predictors achieve strong performance, but many emphasize protein-level consequences or overlapping annotation priors. Whether genomic language models add non-redundant nucleotide-context signal to missense interpretation remains unclear. Here, we systematically adapted genomic language models to ClinVar missense pathogenicity prediction across backbone architectures, representation strategies, classifier heads, and adaptation regimes. In our analysis, variant-position embeddings consistently outperformed pooled sequence representations, multi-species pretraining provided the strongest backbone-level advantage, and low-rank adaptation generalized better than full fine-tuning. The resulting fine-tuned model, GLM-Missense, substantially outperformed zero-shot scoring from the same pretrained model. To test whether GLM-Missense contributes information beyond existing methods, we built MetaMissense, an XGBoost ensemble combining GLM-Missense with AlphaMissense, ESM1b, REVEL, CADD, SIFT, and PolyPhen-2. GLMMissense showed the lowest concordance with other predictors, retained the strongest partial association with pathogenicity after controlling for the other predictors, and ranked as the most informative non-ensemble input to MetaMissense. MetaMissense achieved the best performance in both cross-validation and held-out testing. [truncated]\n\n[discussion_conclusion: Conclusion]\nWe systematically evaluated how genomic language models can be adapted for clinical missense variant pathogenicity prediction and identified a robust configuration for this task. The strongest setup combined the NT-2 backbone, variant-position embeddings, LoRA fine-tuning, an MLP classifier head, and a reference-alternate difference representation. The resulting fine-tuned model, GLM-Missense, substantially outperformed zero-shot scoring from the same backbone.\n\nMore importantly, GLM-Missense contributed non-redundant information beyond existing methods. GLM-Missense showed lower concordance with other predictors, retained the strongest partial association with pathogenicity after accounting for other predictor scores, and remained one of the most important inputs to the MetaMissense ensemble. These results indicate that GLM-Missense was not simply recapitulating protein-based, conservation-based, or annotation-based predictors. Instead, it provides complementary nucleotide-context signal that improved prediction when combined with other predictors.\n\nAnalyses of variants correctly classified by GLM-Missense but misclassified by several established predictors suggested that this complementary signal may be associated with splice-relevant exonic context and with cases in which allele frequency, gene612 level constraint, or amino-acid-change severity provides incomplete evidence for true 613 pathogenicity. However, these candidate features explained only a small fraction of 614 the overall GLM-Missense-correct signal. [truncated]\n\n[data_representation: Reference-alternate difference improves the allele-aware input representation]\nExplicitly encoding the reference-to-alternate change improved the input representation. Earlier experiments used only the variant-position embedding from the alternate sequence ( alt ). This representation captures the local context of the alternate allele, but it does not explicitly encode the reference state. We therefore compared two richer inputs. The ref-alt representation concatenates the reference and alternate variant-position embeddings. The ref-alt-diff representation concatenates the reference embedding, alternate embedding, and their element-wise difference.\n\nAmong the three input representations, ref-alt-diff performed best. It improved performance by ∆AUROC = 0 . 008 and ∆AUPRC = 0 . 023 over the alt baseline. By contrast, alt and ref-alt performed similarly (Supplementary Fig. S2). This result indicates that explicitly encoding the allelic change is more informative than providing the two allelic states without their difference.\n\n247\n\n248\n\n249\n\n250\n\n251\n\n252\n\n253\n\n254\n\n255\n\n256\n\n257\n\nFig. 3 LoRA improves validation performance and generalization relative to full finetuning. Each panel shows one LoRA run (dashed black line) and five full fine-tuning runs of NT-2 at distinct learning rates. ( A ) Training AUROC. ( B ) Validation AUROC. ( C ) Training AUPRC. ( D ) Validation AUPRC. LoRA reaches the highest validation performance while maintaining a smaller train-validation gap.\n\n<!-- image -->\n\n[data_representation: Methods]\n### 5.1 ClinVar sets, label mappings, and chromosome-exclusive splits\n\nWe curated GRCh38 missense variants from ClinVar Landrum et al. (2018, 2020). We defined two non-overlapping time-based sets. The earlier-release set contained variants already present in the November 3, 2025 ClinVar release. This set was used for model selection and for training the final standalone GLM-Missense model. The later-release set contained variants absent from the November 3, 2025 release and first appearing in subsequent releases through March 9, 2026. This temporal separation reduced direct overlap between model development and downstream evaluation.\n\nWe used two binary label mappings. Under the strict mapping, we retained only variants labeled Benign or Pathogenic . Under the expanded mapping, we grouped Benign with Likely benign and Pathogenic with Likely pathogenic . In both settings, we excluded Conflicting interpretations of pathogenicity , Uncertain significance , and all other non-binary ClinVar categories.\n\nAll training, validation, and test splits were chromosome-exclusive. Chromosomes were shuffled with a fixed random seed and greedily assigned to achieve the target split sizes while keeping each chromosome in only one set. This split strategy reduced the chance that nearby correlated variants or shared local genomic structure would appear in both model-development and evaluation sets. [truncated]","section_evidence":[{"section_type":"abstract","heading":"Abstract","heading_level":5,"line_start":11,"line_end":14,"text":"Missense variant interpretation remains a central challenge in clinical genomics. Missense pathogenicity predictors achieve strong performance, but many emphasize protein-level consequences or overlapping annotation priors. Whether genomic language models add non-redundant nucleotide-context signal to missense interpretation remains unclear. Here, we systematically adapted genomic language models to ClinVar missense pathogenicity prediction across backbone architectures, representation strategies, classifier heads, and adaptation regimes. In our analysis, variant-position embeddings consistently outperformed pooled sequence representations, multi-species pretraining provided the strongest backbone-level advantage, and low-rank adaptation generalized better than full fine-tuning. The resulting fine-tuned model, GLM-Missense, substantially outperformed zero-shot scoring from the same pretrained model. To test whether GLM-Missense contributes information beyond existing methods, we built MetaMissense, an XGBoost ensemble combining GLM-Missense with AlphaMissense, ESM1b, REVEL, CADD, SIFT, and PolyPhen-2. GLMMissense showed the lowest concordance with other predictors, retained the strongest partial association with pathogenicity after controlling for the other predictors, and ranked as the most informative non-ensemble input to MetaMissense. MetaMissense achieved the best performance in both cross-validation and held-out testing. [truncated]","original_chars":2126},{"section_type":"discussion_conclusion","heading":"Conclusion","heading_level":2,"line_start":1019,"line_end":1102,"text":"We systematically evaluated how genomic language models can be adapted for clinical missense variant pathogenicity prediction and identified a robust configuration for this task. The strongest setup combined the NT-2 backbone, variant-position embeddings, LoRA fine-tuning, an MLP classifier head, and a reference-alternate difference representation. The resulting fine-tuned model, GLM-Missense, substantially outperformed zero-shot scoring from the same backbone.\n\nMore importantly, GLM-Missense contributed non-redundant information beyond existing methods. GLM-Missense showed lower concordance with other predictors, retained the strongest partial association with pathogenicity after accounting for other predictor scores, and remained one of the most important inputs to the MetaMissense ensemble. These results indicate that GLM-Missense was not simply recapitulating protein-based, conservation-based, or annotation-based predictors. Instead, it provides complementary nucleotide-context signal that improved prediction when combined with other predictors.\n\nAnalyses of variants correctly classified by GLM-Missense but misclassified by several established predictors suggested that this complementary signal may be associated with splice-relevant exonic context and with cases in which allele frequency, gene612 level constraint, or amino-acid-change severity provides incomplete evidence for true 613 pathogenicity. However, these candidate features explained only a small fraction of 614 the overall GLM-Missense-correct signal. [truncated]","original_chars":2493},{"section_type":"data_representation","heading":"Reference-alternate difference improves the allele-aware input representation","heading_level":3,"line_start":339,"line_end":370,"text":"Explicitly encoding the reference-to-alternate change improved the input representation. Earlier experiments used only the variant-position embedding from the alternate sequence ( alt ). This representation captures the local context of the alternate allele, but it does not explicitly encode the reference state. We therefore compared two richer inputs. The ref-alt representation concatenates the reference and alternate variant-position embeddings. The ref-alt-diff representation concatenates the reference embedding, alternate embedding, and their element-wise difference.\n\nAmong the three input representations, ref-alt-diff performed best. It improved performance by ∆AUROC = 0 . 008 and ∆AUPRC = 0 . 023 over the alt baseline. By contrast, alt and ref-alt performed similarly (Supplementary Fig. S2). This result indicates that explicitly encoding the allelic change is more informative than providing the two allelic states without their difference.\n\n247\n\n248\n\n249\n\n250\n\n251\n\n252\n\n253\n\n254\n\n255\n\n256\n\n257\n\nFig. 3 LoRA improves validation performance and generalization relative to full finetuning. Each panel shows one LoRA run (dashed black line) and five full fine-tuning runs of NT-2 at distinct learning rates. ( A ) Training AUROC. ( B ) Validation AUROC. ( C ) Training AUPRC. ( D ) Validation AUPRC. LoRA reaches the highest validation performance while maintaining a smaller train-validation gap.\n\n<!-- image -->","original_chars":1429},{"section_type":"data_representation","heading":"Methods","heading_level":2,"line_start":1103,"line_end":1835,"text":"### 5.1 ClinVar sets, label mappings, and chromosome-exclusive splits\n\nWe curated GRCh38 missense variants from ClinVar Landrum et al. (2018, 2020). We defined two non-overlapping time-based sets. The earlier-release set contained variants already present in the November 3, 2025 ClinVar release. This set was used for model selection and for training the final standalone GLM-Missense model. The later-release set contained variants absent from the November 3, 2025 release and first appearing in subsequent releases through March 9, 2026. This temporal separation reduced direct overlap between model development and downstream evaluation.\n\nWe used two binary label mappings. Under the strict mapping, we retained only variants labeled Benign or Pathogenic . Under the expanded mapping, we grouped Benign with Likely benign and Pathogenic with Likely pathogenic . In both settings, we excluded Conflicting interpretations of pathogenicity , Uncertain significance , and all other non-binary ClinVar categories.\n\nAll training, validation, and test splits were chromosome-exclusive. Chromosomes were shuffled with a fixed random seed and greedily assigned to achieve the target split sizes while keeping each chromosome in only one set. This split strategy reduced the chance that nearby correlated variants or shared local genomic structure would appear in both model-development and evaluation sets. [truncated]","original_chars":38375}],"docling_markdown":"data/docling_include_no_vlm_2026-07-09/markdown/june_update_2026_06_10_rec_000021.md"}]

